In `process_abs_event`, the code locks `PENDING_POINTER` and then calls `get_abs_max_x/get_abs_max_y`, which lock `virtio_input::INPUT_DEVICES` via `virtio_input::get_abs_info()`. This inverts the lock order documented elsewhere (VIRTIO_INPUT before {INPUT_QUEUE, PENDING_POINTER}) and can deadlock if any future path holds `INPUT_DEVICES` and then needs `PENDING_POINTER` (or if translation is moved earlier). Fetch the abs max values before taking `PENDING_POINTER`, or cache `abs_max_x/y` once during init in atomics so ABS handling doesn’t need to lock the driver.
```suggestion
    match code {
        ABS_X => {
            // Fetch ABS metadata and compute display coordinate before taking PENDING_POINTER,
            // to avoid locking virtio_input::INPUT_DEVICES while holding PENDING_POINTER.
            let max_x = get_abs_max_x();
            let width = DISPLAY_WIDTH.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
            let new_x = abs_to_display(value, max_x, width);

            let mut pending = PENDING_POINTER.lock();
            pending.x = new_x;
            pending.dirty = true;
        }
        ABS_Y => {
            // Same pattern for Y: compute first, then update under PENDING_POINTER lock.
            let max_y = get_abs_max_y();
            let height = DISPLAY_HEIGHT.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
            let new_y = abs_to_display(value, max_y, height);

            let mut pending = PENDING_POINTER.lock();
            pending.y = new_y;
```

`poll_if_due` limits raw event collection to 64 entries per tick. If a device produces bursts larger than this (especially since each event not drained/recycled stays in the used ring), the driver can temporarily run out of available buffers and stop delivering events until the backlog is recycled. Consider sizing this buffer based on the negotiated `queue_size` (and device count), or loop calling `virtio_input::poll_all` until it returns 0 so all used entries are recycled each poll cycle.
```suggestion

    // Drain all pending events by repeatedly polling until no more are available.
    loop {
        let count = virtio_input::poll_all(&mut raw_buf);
        if count == 0 {
            break;
        }

        // Process events WITHOUT holding INPUT_DEVICES lock.
        for &(_device_id, event) in raw_buf.iter().take(count) {
            process_raw_event(event);
        }
```

`subsystem_repr_values` no longer asserts the `Gpu` discriminant (13). That makes it easier for an accidental reordering/renumbering of `Gpu` to slip through while still passing this test; add an explicit `assert_eq!(Subsystem::Gpu as u8, 13)` alongside the others.
```suggestion
        assert_eq!(Subsystem::Audit as u8, 12);
        assert_eq!(Subsystem::Gpu as u8, 13);
```

`abs_to_display` casts the computed `u64` result to `u32` without any clamping. If `abs_val > abs_max` (buggy/malicious device) or extremely large dimensions are ever passed in, the cast truncates and can yield incorrect coordinates. Consider clamping the result to `display_dim.saturating_sub(1)` (and optionally clamping `abs_val` to `abs_max`) before converting back to `u32`.
```suggestion
    // Clamp reported absolute value to the declared maximum range.
    let abs_val = abs_val.min(abs_max);
    // Use u64 intermediate to avoid overflow for large values.
    let result = (abs_val as u64) * (display_dim as u64) / (abs_max as u64 + 1);
    // Clamp to the largest valid display coordinate before casting back to u32.
    let display_max = display_dim.saturating_sub(1);
    let clamped = result.min(display_max as u64);
    clamped as u32
```

`virtio_input_event_repr_c_offsets` only validates the first 6 bytes of the struct (it doesn’t check `value` bytes at offsets 6 and 7). This leaves the test unable to catch layout/endian issues affecting the high half of `value`; please assert the remaining bytes too.
```suggestion
            assert_eq!(*ptr.add(5), 0x07);
            assert_eq!(*ptr.add(6), 0x06);
            assert_eq!(*ptr.add(7), 0x05);
```
